    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0376a051-e535-5449-913f-e1f42f2b6120",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Complete the square on $x^2-5x+\\frac{17}{4}$.\n\nHalve the linear coefficient, then add and subtract the square of that half. Fill in the nonnegative numbers.\n\n$x^2-5x+\\frac{17}{4}=(x-$ {{field-1}} $)^2-$ {{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{5}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2-5x+\\frac{17}{4}&=\\underbrace{x^2-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+\\frac{25}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{25}{4}+\\frac{17}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{25}{4}+\\frac{17}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-5x$.\n\nThe blanks are $\\frac{5}{2}$ and $2$; the sign before the second blank is already supplied.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "56535993-356f-5e43-9adc-ca6734e194ea",
