      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Rewrite $\\frac{29}{4}-7x+x^2$ in the form $(x+a)^2+b$. Find the signed numbers $a$ and $b$.\n\n$a=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$b=$ {{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put the quadratic term first: $\\frac{29}{4}-7x+x^2=x^2-7x+\\frac{29}{4}$.\n\nThe half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{7}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2-7x+\\frac{29}{4}&=\\underbrace{x^2-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{49}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{49}{4}+\\frac{29}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{49}{4}+\\frac{29}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{7}{2}\\right)^2-5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-7x$.\n\nComparing with $(x+a)^2+b$ gives $a=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $b=-5$.",
